<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jura Mountains</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Jura Mountains are a sub-alpine range of folded limestone running about 300 kilometres in an arc north of the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>, along the border of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>. Their valleys and plateaus were home to the Sequani and Raurici in antiquity, and later divided between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>, the Prince-Bishopric of Basel and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neuchatel">Neuchâtel</a>. Watchmaking became their characteristic industry, and the range gave its name to the Jurassic geological period.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jura-mountains.jpg" alt="Jura Mountains" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was Cyr at English Wikipedia. · Attribution · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jura_Mountains.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Jura range proper, the folded Jura, lies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> and mainly marks a long part of their border. To the northeast it continues as the Table Jura, which is not folded, through northern Switzerland and into the German states of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden-wurttemberg">Baden-Württemberg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, and is crossed by the High Rhine. In France the range covers most of the Franche-Comté region and reaches its highest point at the Crêt de la Neige, at 1,720 metres, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of Ain. About 1,600 square kilometres of it are protected as the Jura Mountains Regional Natural Park.</p><p>The range is built of a sequence of geological folds, formed as the Alpine mountain building pushed northwards. Three bands date from the Lias, Dogger and Malm, the Early, Middle and Late Jurassic. The folds are young enough to show little erosion. The range separates the basins of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhone">Rhône</a>: the north and east drain to the Rhine through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aare">Aare</a> and the Ill, the west and south to the Rhône through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/doubs">Doubs</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saone">Saône</a> and the Ain.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The name is first recorded as mons Iura in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a>'s Commentarii de Bello Gallico, and the geographer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> gives a Greek form of it. Early Celtic scholars proposed that it was a Celtic word for mountains, but there are no clear cognates in the surviving Celtic languages, and its origin remains unclear. The range gave its name to the French department of Jura, the Swiss canton of Jura, the Jurassic period of the geological timescale and the Montes Jura on the Moon.</p><p>Much of the Swiss Jura had no historical association with early modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> and was incorporated into the Swiss Confederacy only in the 19th century. In the 20th century a separatist movement developed there, which led to the creation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Canton</a> of Jura in 1979. The Swiss Jura has been industrialised since the 18th century and became a major centre of watchmaking. High-lying towns such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-chaux-de-fonds">La Chaux-de-Fonds</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/le-locle">Le Locle</a> and Sainte-Croix, known for its musical boxes, grew up there. In the French Jura the 11th-century Fort de Joux, near Pontarlier, was remodelled and strengthened by Vauban in 1690.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The range offers hiking, cycling and skiing, with signposted trails such as the 310-kilometre Jura ridgeway. Swiss attractions include the Creux du Van, the Chasseral, the Grottes de Vallorbe and the Taubenloch gorge. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/le-locle">Le Locle</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-chaux-de-fonds">La Chaux-de-Fonds</a> are together a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> for their watchmaking heritage.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jura Mountains” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 888 the Welf Rudolf I had been crowned at the abbey of Saint-Maurice d’Agaune as king of Upper Burgundy, the lands between the <b>Jura</b> and the Pennine Alps around Lake Geneva.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-arles">Kingdom of Arles · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Montbéliard guarded the Belfort Gap between the <b>Jura</b> and the Vosges.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-montbeliard">County of Montbéliard</a></li><li><blockquote>Tacitus says the Helvetii once lived between the Rhine and the Main before moving to the Swiss plateau between the <b>Jura</b> and the Alps.</blockquote><a href="/state/helvetii">Helvetii</a></li><li><blockquote>It traded across the <b>Jura</b> and the Saône valley.</blockquote><a href="/state/imperial-city-of-besancon">Imperial City of Besançon</a></li><li><blockquote>His kingdom covered western Switzerland, the <b>Jura</b> and parts of Franche-Comté, and he held it against the East Frankish king Arnulf.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-upper-burgundy">Kingdom of Upper Burgundy</a></li><li><blockquote>In 999 King Rudolf III of Burgundy gave the bishop the abbey of Moutier-Grandval in the <b>Jura</b>, the basis of later territory.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-basel">Prince-Bishopric of Basel</a></li></ul></section>
